444	- Do not use script src, link href, module imports, fetch, XMLHttpRequest, WebSocket, iframe, object, or embed.
445	- The code must be self-contained and run directly inside a sandboxed iframe.
446	- Use SVG or Canvas for the main visualization.
447	- Use input controls such as sliders, buttons, toggles, or draggable elements when useful.
448	
449	System-provided base CSS will be injected into <head> after your answer.
450	Use these classes for layout and visual quality instead of writing broad CSS boilerplate:
451	- iv-shell, iv-header, iv-kicker, iv-title, iv-subtitle
452	- iv-layout, iv-card, iv-visual-card, iv-controls-card, iv-observation-card
453	- iv-section-title, iv-control, iv-control-row, iv-label, iv-value
454	- iv-note, iv-conclusion, iv-formula, iv-pill, iv-badge
455	You may include a small inline <style> for concept-specific SVG/Canvas geometry, animation, and labels.
456	Do not duplicate large CSS resets, font stacks, card systems, design tokens, or generic body styling.
457	
458	Design requirements:
459	- The visual must be clean, modern, and suitable for teaching.
460	- Use a clear layout with a title area, core visualization area, control area, and observation/conclusion area.
461	- Important changes must be visually obvious.
462	- Use color, motion, labels, guides, and annotations to explain the concept.
463	- Avoid clutter.
464	- Make it responsive.
465	- Use the system classes for rounded cards, soft shadows, readable typography, and sufficient spacing.
466	- Do not make a dashboard-style page with too many large cards. The visual should be the first thing learners notice.
